{% endif %}{% if scorecard_table %}## Scorecard

Every model is scored with the same functions on real rows that it did not learn from. A score means something only
between two reference tables of the same size. The floor (independent columns) keeps each column's distribution and
destroys every relation between columns, so it shows what the marginals alone give. The ceiling (real rows) is a fresh
sample of the training data, so it shows what a perfect generator would score. For privacy the ceiling is the worst case,
because it copies real rows.

